<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Elisyces</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 200 BCE (approx.) · capital Naro</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Iberian-influenced people of the coastal plain around Narbonne, among the first inhabitants of Gaul named in Greek writing.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Known only from a handful of Greek sources. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hecataeus-of-miletus">Hecataeus of Miletus</a> names them around 500 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> lists Elisyces among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> hired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> for the Battle of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/himera">Himera</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 480 BCE. They held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> coast between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herault-river">Hérault</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montlaures">Naro</a>, the oppidum of Montlaurès beside <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/viscounty-of-narbonne">Narbonne</a>, as their main centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Archaeology suggests a mixed culture with strong Iberian features: Iberian script on pottery and lead tablets, fortified hilltop settlements such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enserune">Ensérune</a>, and busy exchange with Greek <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/massalia">Massalia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emporion">Emporion</a> in wine, painted pottery and metals.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The name fades from the record by the 3rd century BCE, when Celtic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volcae">Volcae</a> dominated the region. In 118 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> founded the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbo Martius</a> next to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montlaures">Montlaurès</a>, which became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/elisyces.jpg" alt="Elisyces" width="320" height="378"><figcaption>Image: User:Cristiano64 (Transferred by User:Coldeel/Originally uploaded by Cristiano64 (it)) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaule_-54.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 200 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Naro</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Iberian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-350&amp;focus=elisyces" aria-label="Elisyces on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5025 835 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Elisyces lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5345 1091h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5345 1091h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Naro. <a href="/atlas?year=-350&amp;focus=elisyces">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

